MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi, Phil.

Can I know the intraclass correlation (ICC) in mixedmirt() products? If can I, How to estimate it?

Seongho

From ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com  Tue Mar 14 20:39:39 2017
From: ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com (Phil Chalmers)
Date: Tue, 14 Mar 2017 20:39:39 +0000
Subject: Re: Can I know the intraclass correlation (ICC) in mixedmirt() products?
Message-ID: <4D0dy46Q_Qc.NmzoaAwMEgAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <4D0dy46Q_Qc.fzSr5lunBAAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
References: <4D0dy46Q_Qc.fzSr5lunBAAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/4D0dy46Q_Qc/m/NmzoaAwMEgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It's just the ratio of relevant nested variance terms. So if you have a group variance and an individual observation variance term then ICC = var_g / (var_g + var_i) w.r.t the group cluster. HTH.

Phil

